1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,170
This tutorial shows how to make the aluminum cups and plugs that carry powder into the rePowder atomizer.

2
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:17,960
Each cup is turned from 3/4 inch 6063 aluminum bar, filled with alloy powder, and closed with a plug that has a small air hole through it.

3
00:00:18,310 --> 00:00:27,814
You'll see the first cups being made on the lathe, the same steps from the CAD, the numbers to use, and what changes for the hydraulic-press versions.

4
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:31,350
First, where they end up.

5
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:36,596
The cups stand plug-up in the graphite crucible, around the sealing rod.

6
00:00:36,946 --> 00:00:43,474
Standing plug-up means the powder drops into metal that's already liquid, and the air hole points up.

7
00:00:44,617 --> 00:00:55,585
Everything is turned from this bar: 3/4 inch 6063 aluminum rod, McMaster part 1640T16, sold in 2 foot lengths.

8
00:00:55,935 --> 00:01:00,423
A cup and its plug together use about 3.5 inches of bar.

9
00:01:00,773 --> 00:01:05,573
Check what's left before you start, and reorder if it's running short.

10
00:01:06,717 --> 00:01:09,477
Here's how the first cups were made.

11
00:01:09,827 --> 00:01:17,363
Gage marked the bar at about 3 inches per cup: a quarter inch over the finished length, so both ends can be faced.

12
00:01:19,167 --> 00:01:23,867
“We're going to cut this on the band saw [into] pieces, and then we're going to need to drill out on the lathe the interior hole.”

13
00:01:23,867 --> 00:01:32,367
“And then we'll also turn out on the lathe the... plugs.”

14
00:01:33,050 --> 00:01:42,290
The blanks were cut on the band saw: three pieces 3 inches long, plus a piece about 1 3/8 inches long for the plugs.

15
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,600
“Poke it just a little bit, that's all you need, huh? / You just need a guide hole.”

16
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:52,700
“So we just use the center drill, poke out your hole, and we'll put our 1/2-inch drill in.”

17
00:01:52,967 --> 00:02:00,267
“This is the first one: we cut the ends so they're flat on both sides, and now this is perfectly at 2.75 inches.”

18
00:02:00,983 --> 00:02:08,615
The half-inch drill then went in 2.25 inches deep. The drill tip leaves a pointed bottom, and that's fine.

19
00:02:08,965 --> 00:02:12,037
Each finished cup went into its own bag.

20
00:02:13,183 --> 00:02:18,535
Here are the same steps from the CAD. One: face the end of the bar.

21
00:02:18,885 --> 00:02:26,853
Two: center drill, then drill the pocket. The first cups used a 1/2 inch drill, 2.25 inches deep.

22
00:02:27,203 --> 00:02:31,331
Drilling is shown cut in half, so you can see inside.

23
00:02:31,681 --> 00:02:39,697
The drawing follows a 31/64 drill with a reamer or a boring bar, for a smooth, round hole on size.

24
00:02:40,047 --> 00:02:44,511
The first cups skipped that step, but the press versions will want it.

25
00:02:44,861 --> 00:02:50,621
Three: part it off. The cups so far are 2.75 inches long.

26
00:02:50,971 --> 00:02:58,267
That's the cup: a 1/2 inch hole, 2.25 inches deep, a 1/8 inch wall, and a solid bottom.

27
00:02:59,417 --> 00:03:03,209
Now the plug. The CAD calls it the lid.

28
00:03:03,559 --> 00:03:13,039
Make the cup first, then its plug. Measure that cup's hole, and turn the plug 0.0005 to 0.0008 inches bigger, so it presses in.

29
00:03:13,389 --> 00:03:19,581
Taper the nose slightly. That's the end that goes in, and the taper starts it in straight.

30
00:03:19,931 --> 00:03:24,467
Drill the air hole straight through the middle, with a #60 drill.

31
00:03:24,817 --> 00:03:30,553
Break the top edge. It's a deburr only, and plays no part in the fit.

32
00:03:30,903 --> 00:03:36,039
Part it off. The plugs so far are 9/16 inch long.

33
00:03:36,389 --> 00:03:44,477
One plug per cup, turned to that cup's measured hole. The first plugs came out at 0.508 inch diameter.

34
00:03:44,827 --> 00:03:48,667
Measure every hole, rather than reusing that number.

35
00:03:49,817 --> 00:03:57,497
The #60 drill for the air hole is in the wire-gauge drill drawer, next to the #70 used on the graphite nozzle.

36
00:03:57,847 --> 00:04:00,655
Ask a lab assistant to open it.

37
00:04:01,817 --> 00:04:05,417
Filling comes later, but it explains the hole.

38
00:04:05,767 --> 00:04:12,967
Tap the powder down to the line where the plug will sit, and weigh what goes in: about 8.7 grams per cup.

39
00:04:13,317 --> 00:04:18,141
A caliper depth rod set to 9/16 inch marks the line.

40
00:04:18,491 --> 00:04:20,987
Then the plug goes in flush.

41
00:04:21,337 --> 00:04:24,361
The chamber is pumped down before melting.

42
00:04:24,711 --> 00:04:31,935
With the hole, the air under the plug has somewhere to go. Without it, the air has to come out through the powder.

43
00:04:33,083 --> 00:04:35,771
Here are the numbers in one place.

44
00:04:36,121 --> 00:04:41,377
Keep making cups the way the first ones were made, so the runs stay comparable.

45
00:04:41,727 --> 00:04:54,159
Cups 2.75 inches long, with a 1/2 inch hole 2.25 inches deep. Plugs 9/16 inch long, with a #60 air hole, sized to each cup.

46
00:04:54,509 --> 00:04:59,117
The drawing's numbers are the original design, shown for reference.

47
00:05:00,283 --> 00:05:02,155
Four rules.

48
00:05:02,505 --> 00:05:12,777
One: make the cup first, then its plug, sized to that cup. Never more than 0.0008 inches over: tighter than that permanently stretches the cup wall.

49
00:05:13,127 --> 00:05:17,471
Two: every plug gets an air hole, straight through the middle.

50
00:05:17,821 --> 00:05:22,501
Three: taper the plug's leading end, so it starts in straight.

51
00:05:22,851 --> 00:05:28,227
Four: keep each plug bagged with its own cup. They aren't interchangeable.

52
00:05:28,577 --> 00:05:38,993
Don't mark, scribe or stamp the parts. Wipe them with isopropyl alcohol, let them dry, weigh each one to 0.01 gram, and label the bag.

53
00:05:40,150 --> 00:05:45,694
Some cups will be compacted in the hydraulic press, and those change in three ways.

54
00:05:46,044 --> 00:05:51,084
First, the plug has to slide in and out of the cup, rather than press in.

55
00:05:51,434 --> 00:06:01,586
Second, the inside of the cup should be fairly clean and smooth. It doesn't have to be perfect: drill undersize, then ream or bore to size.

56
00:06:01,936 --> 00:06:11,080
Third, the plug has to be able to travel down to the powder and compact it, so the hole must be straight, and the same size all the way down.

57
00:06:12,217 --> 00:06:20,425
The full drawing, the CAD and these animations are in pull request 232, linked from issue 222.

58
00:06:20,775 --> 00:06:27,687
The lengths on the drawing are design values. For the standard cups, go by the numbers in this video.

59
00:06:28,037 --> 00:06:36,413
One request: nobody has filmed the plugs being turned yet. Please record that step this time, with the chest camera.

60
00:06:37,550 --> 00:06:40,406
Links to everything are in the description.

